+4.2 The explicit symmetrizer value of $c_T$. We use [F2] only for the row parabolic, localized at differences of variables in the same type-row interval. This restriction is essential: differences between arbitrary JM entries need not be invertible on a Specht module. The chain-$T$ subspace of step 2.2 is stable under the row parabolic; its JM eigenwords are precisely the standard refinements of $T$, so the entries in each type row occupy a horizontal strip in each physical component. Two nodes of a horizontal strip in one component have distinct contents: for upper row $i$ and lower row $j$, every strip node of row $i$ lies strictly right of every strip node of row $j$, making the contents strictly ordered. For different components the content difference is at most $n-2$ (each node has absolute content bounded by its component size minus one), so it cannot cancel the nonzero multiple $n(a-b)$ in the deformation; in the universal field the independent $U_a$ separate them. Thus every same-row variable difference acts invertibly on this entire chain subspace. The finite row Bernstein relations therefore extend its action to this row-local algebra. The crossed-product coefficient calculation below is an identity in that row-local algebra and can be evaluated here, without extending the action to the localization at all variable differences. For a row interval $I$, put $x_I=\sum_{w\in S_I}T_w$, write $x_I=\sum_wa_ww$; the row-coset identity $T_ix_I=zx_I$ gives $a_{s_iw}=s_i(a_w)$ by coefficient comparison with $T_i=c_{i,i+1}+d_{i,i+1}s_i$, and the coefficient of the longest permutation comes from the product of the $d_{ij}=(X_j-zX_i)/(X_j-X_i)$ over the positive roots of $I$, each once. Hence $a_1=w_0\bigl(\prod_{i<j\in I}\frac{X_j-zX_i}{X_j-X_i}\bigr)=\prod_{i<j\in I}\frac{zX_j-X_i}{X_j-X_i}=C_I(X)$. Disjoint row intervals commute, so $x_\nu=\prod_Ix_I$ has identity coefficient $\prod_IC_I(X)$; expanding $x_I=C_I(X)+\sum_{w\ne1}b_w(X)\Phi_w$ and using that every $\Phi